WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.510
About 500 episodes in, Pawn Stars still attracts
a dedicated audience of history buffs and

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:06.510
hagglers.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:10.960
While the show presents itself like a documentary
series about a genuine high-end pawn shop,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.540
there's a lot that goes on behind the scenes
that the cameras just ignore.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.550
So, what's the truth about what really happens
at the Gold &amp; Silver Pawn Shop?

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.170
Bad for business

00:00:21.170 --> 00:00:25.770
When the crew shoots an episode of Pawn Stars,
parts of the World Famous Gold &amp; Silver Pawn

00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:30.189
Shop temporarily close to the public, which
can sometimes mean a real interruption of

00:00:30.189 --> 00:00:31.680
business.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.690
According to shop manager Travis Benton, sales
to real customers suffer because of the show,

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:40.850
since filming takes place every weekday, two
to five times per day.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:45.449
Benton also estimated that "one out of a hundred"
people who pass through the shop are actually

00:00:45.449 --> 00:00:46.940
there to do business.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:49.690
The rest are just there as fans of the show.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:53.870
Rick Harrison backed that up big time in an
interview with Las Vegas Now, claiming his

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:54.870
shop

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.940
"...competes with the 'Welcome to Las Vegas'
sign for the number one non-gaming tourist

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.519
attraction in Las Vegas."

00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:03.510
And it seems to be true.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.710
Lines routinely extend down the sidewalk,
and sources report that they can sometimes

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:10.240
take up to three hours to get through.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.080
But visitors should should also be aware that
if they're waiting hours to catch a glimpse

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:17.370
of their favorite Pawn Star, their chances
aren't great.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.590
Traveller reports,

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:22.450
"Occasionally one of the superstars will come
out from the back (where they produce the

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:26.190
show every working day) and sign autographs
for about an hour."

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:30.160
But before you start thinking that the shop
is in danger of going out of business because

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.260
most of their customers are just there to
gawk, keep in mind that the store draws 4,000

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.690
to 5,000 visitors a day.

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:39.870
Not to mention Harrison told Las Vegas Now,

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:43.920
"I'm at a point in my life where, if I don't
like it, I don't have to do it."

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.060
And Pawn Stars isn't super famous in just
America, either.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.950
An international hit

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:55.370
Having now been translated into 38 languages,
and aired in 150 countries, Pawn Stars is

00:01:55.370 --> 00:02:01.240
a bonafide international hit.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:05.630
In fact, Harrison claims he's currently suffering
the pains of super-stardom.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:11.670
"[I've] been mobbed by people in Buenos Aires,
the Philippines, Kuala Lumpur, New Delhi.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.519
Everywhere in the world, people know the show."

00:02:13.519 --> 00:02:19.269
The guys even did an Asian tour in 2013, during
which Las Vegas Now said they were, quote,

00:02:19.269 --> 00:02:22.180
"greeted as if they were the reunited Beatles."

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.480
And according to OKJ Discoveries, fans lined
up for hours at various meet-and-greet events

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:33.209
attended by Harrison and his son, Corey "Big
Hoss" Harrison, all just for the chance to

00:02:33.209 --> 00:02:35.400
lay eyes upon the reality stars.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.470
It's pretty remarkable to think that a TV
show about old junk could turn a couple of

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:44.739
guys from a pawn shop into megastars on par
with rock and roll legends.

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:46.779
The Pawn Stars pitch

00:02:46.779 --> 00:02:50.599
Rick Harrison says he always knew TV exposure
was good for business.

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:56.030
That's why he starred in a 2001 PBS documentary
and agreed to be on Comedy Central's Insomniac

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:01.890
in 2003 before coming up with the idea to
pitch an entire show based on the daily goings-on

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:02.890
of his shop.

00:03:02.890 --> 00:03:08.159
The original concept for Pawn Stars started
on HBO, but after a disastrous pilot and the

00:03:08.159 --> 00:03:12.939
expiration of his contract, Harrison turned
to Leftfield Productions, the company that

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:15.180
still produces the show today.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.560
Harrison told The Star-Telegram,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.840
"I figured that a show would mean free publicity
and free publicity would mean more business.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.400
But everyone told me that no one wants to
watch a show about four fat guys in a pawnshop."

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.269
Millions of dollars in profits later, whoever
turned Harrison down couldn't have been more

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:32.269
wrong.

00:03:32.269 --> 00:03:34.189
A wild Chumlee appears

00:03:34.189 --> 00:03:39.379
Despite his legal troubles, Chumlee is far
and away the fan favorite of Pawn Stars.

00:03:39.379 --> 00:03:44.029
Even Harrison knows this, as he once told
The Star-Telegram that tourists often opt

00:03:44.029 --> 00:03:45.030
for the Chumlee merchandise.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:48.029
"I put my pants on two legs at a time."

00:03:48.029 --> 00:03:50.059
But Chumlee wasn't always part of the plan.

00:03:50.059 --> 00:03:54.529
He told Las Vegas Now that the show was originally
just a family gig until they started looking

00:03:54.529 --> 00:03:56.310
for a fourth cast member, saying,

00:03:56.310 --> 00:04:00.579
"...they were looking for a fourth person
to put in the show, and I thought to myself,

00:04:00.579 --> 00:04:03.029
well, I've known these guys my whole life.

00:04:03.029 --> 00:04:06.830
We have good camaraderie, and I'm going to
be funny and joke with them like we would

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:11.059
normally do, and not get nervous in front
of the cameras, and it kind of worked out."

00:04:11.059 --> 00:04:14.699
And by "kind of worked out" he means he became
a multi-millionaire.

00:04:14.699 --> 00:04:16.530
"See ya later dork."

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:18.400
Sued by their agents

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.270
The pawn business is a dirty game, but the
entertainment industry is worse, especially

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:25.810
in the cutthroat world of celebrity representation.

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:32.320
In 2012, all three Harrisons as well as Chumlee
were sued by their ex-manager Wayne Jefferies,

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.650
who claimed that the Pawn Stars crew dropped
him after a dust-up concerning his handling

00:04:36.650 --> 00:04:41.020
of their response to A&amp;E's spinoff show, Cajun
Pawn Stars.

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:46.380
History Channel was also sued by a talent
agency called Venture IAB, whose lawsuit alleged

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:51.080
that network execs worked with another agent
to lure the guys away from them.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:57.370
Jefferies sought "unspecified damages," but
Venture IAB sued for "for more than $5 million."

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:01.120
Harrison didn't seem to be fazed by either
lawsuit, telling Fox News,

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.510
"The more money you make, the more times people
sue you.

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:08.710
People are always going to…eventually go
to sue you for something."

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.680
And when it comes to defending himself in
court, we're sure he's got a guy for that.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:13.850
Thanks for watching!

